## Bell Numbers: Counting Set Partitions
Bell numbers are a sequence of numbers that appear in combinatorial mathematics, and they count the number of partitions of a set. A partition of a set is a way of dividing the set into non-empty, disjoint subsets.

### Quick Diagram

Consider a set with 3 elements: {a, b, c}. The partitions of this set are:

1. {a}, {b}, {c} (3 subsets)
2. {a, b}, {c} (2 subsets)
3. {a, c}, {b} (2 subsets)
4. {b, c}, {a} (2 subsets)
5. {a, b, c} (1 subset)

There are 5 partitions of the set {a, b, c}. This is the 3rd Bell number, B(3) = 5.

### Java Function

Here is a Java function that calculates the nth Bell number:
```java
public class BellNumbers {
    public static long bellNumber(int n) {
        long[] bell = new long[n + 1];
        bell[0] = 1;

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            bell[i] = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                bell[i] += bell[j] * combination(i - 1, j);
            }
        }

        return bell[n];
    }

    public static long combination(int n, int k) {
        if (k > n - k) {
            k = n - k;
        }

        long result = 1;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
            result = result * (n - i) / (i + 1);
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        for (int i = 0; i <= 5; i++) {
            System.out.println("B(" + i + ") = " + bellNumber(i));
        }
    }
}
```
